{
  "dom_id": "step-q212079",
  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Consider the set $B=\\{1,2,3\\}$ and the relation $R=\\{(x,y)\\in {B}^{2}\\,:\\,x+y=3\\}.$\nFill in the missing values in the adjacency matrix of the relation:\n\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | {{field-1}} | $0$ |\n| $2$ | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |\n| $3$ | $0$ | {{field-5}} | $0$ |",
  "worked_solution": "If a relation $R$ is defined over $A=\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{m}\\}$ and $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},…{b}_{n}\\}$ of cardinalities $m$ and $n,$ respectively, the *adjacency matrix* of $R$ is a $m\\times n$-matrix (table), where the element ${r}_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column is given by\n\n$$\n{r}_{ij}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,\\text{if }{a}_{i}\\,R\\,{b}_{j}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {B}^{2}$ with $x+y=3.$\n\nLet's consider each row in our table.\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the value $y=2$ satisfies $x+y=3.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(1,2),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $2$nd column in the adjacency matrix.\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $x+y=3.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(2,1),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $2$nd row and the $1$st column in the adjacency matrix.\n\n- If $x=3,$ then there is no value of $y\\in B$ that satisfies $x+y=3.$\n\nAll the other cells in the matrix must be filled with zeros. Therefore, we have the following adjacency matrix:\n\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $3$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
